=> What does "debugging" mean?

Debugging means finding out why a script is not working as expected.

For example, suppose you create this script:

#!/bin/bash

NAME="Bilal"

echo "Hello $NAME"

mkdir /backup

echo "Backup completed"

You run it:

./script.sh

But you get an error:

mkdir: cannot create directory '/backup': Permission denied
Backup completed

You may wonder:

Which command caused the problem?

This is where debugging helps.

=> bash -x script.sh

The easiest way to debug a Bash script is:

bash -x script.sh

The -x means:

Show me each command before Bash executes it.

Example

Create:

nano test.sh

Program:

#!/bin/bash

NAME="Bilal"

echo "Hello $NAME"

mkdir /backup

echo "Backup completed"

Output:

Hello Bilal
mkdir: cannot create directory '/backup': Permission denied
Backup completed

=> It's not immediately obvious what Bash is doing internally.

Now run:

bash -x test.sh

You may see:

+ NAME=Bilal
+ echo 'Hello Bilal'
Hello Bilal
+ mkdir /backup
mkdir: cannot create directory '/backup': Permission denied
+ echo 'Backup completed'
Backup completed

So you can easily identify:

+ mkdir /backup
mkdir: cannot create directory '/backup': Permission denied

Now you know:

The problem happened when Bash executed mkdir /backup.

=> set -x

Instead of debugging the whole script from outside, you can enable debugging inside the script.

Example:

#!/bin/bash

NAME="Bilal"

set -x

echo "Hello $NAME"

mkdir /backup

echo "Backup completed"

Output:

+ echo 'Hello Bilal'
Hello Bilal
+ mkdir /backup
mkdir: cannot create directory '/backup': Permission denied
+ echo 'Backup completed'
Backup completed

=> set +x

What if you don't want debugging for the entire script?

You can turn debugging off using:

set +x

Program:

#!/bin/bash

NAME="Bilal"

set -x

echo "Starting script"

echo "Hello $NAME"

set +x

echo "This command will not be shown with +"
echo "Script completed"

So you can debug only a particular part of your script.
